  "dimensions": [
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_85d09c11",
      "@type": "OverallIntent",
      "instance_name": "overallintent_instance",
      "label": "the overallintent",
      "description": "meme-id: the_lie_detector_test_meme; hasOverallIntent: Harmful; overall-intent-description: The image depicts a man speaking at a podium with text overlay that reads 'the lie detector test determined that was a lie.' This is a satirical or ironic meme, likely referencing a moment where someone is caught in a falsehood, and the lie detector test is used as a humorous or exaggerated tool to 'prove' the lie. The visual element of the man (often identified as actor John Ritter or a similar figure in a comedic context) combined with the text creates an analogy where the lie detector test is being used to expose deception. The meme's intent is to mock or highlight the absurdity of a situation where a lie is being 'detected' by a device, often used to critique dishonesty or to humorously frame a statement as false. There is no direct targeting of any specific group based on protected characteristics, so it does not fall under abusive, cyberbullying, hateful, misogynous, or propaganda categories. However, it may be considered harmful if it is used to undermine someone's credibility or to spread misinformation in a context where the lie detector test is being misrepresented or used to discredit a person or argument. The meme relies on the juxtaposition of the serious-sounding 'lie detector test' with the comedic or ironic tone of the situation, creating a humorous effect that may be interpreted as mocking or ridiculing the subject of the lie.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_861d1c36"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
